{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1434 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "53e6d10ae98a81e853c9a22415068279c3db6ef45afd1592ca3566bce6c107aa" :edb/db-before-t 1433 :edb/db-after-t 1434 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534746 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:44:29.982Z" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054348 1100 "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\}$, $B = \\{f,g\\}$, and $C = A \\times B$, determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n1. $(f,4) \\in C$\n2. $(7,g) \\in C$\n3. $(3,f) \\in C$" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054348 1100 "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\},$ $B = \\{f,g\\},$ and $C = A \\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(f,4) \\in C$\n\n- $(7,g) \\in C$\n\n- $(3,f) \\in C$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054348 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/488cc12c3ff8d8b797a5ecd57846bd45.png)\n\nHence, the set\n\n$$\nC = A \\times B\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{\\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054348 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/683bad9c822cea7218b2fd8a252e46215103c95b3ee9c42e41e7141ed8a40330.png)\n\nHence, the set $C = A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{ \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054349 1100 "If $A = \\{ p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4,9 \\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B?$\n\n- $((p,4),9)$\n\n- $((q,9),4)$\n\n- $(p,4,9)$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054349 1100 "If $A = \\{p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{4,9 \\}$, which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B$?\n\n1. $((p,4),9)$\n2. $((q,9),4)$\n3. $(p,4,9)$" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054349 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{p,q \\}$ with $\\{4,9 \\}$, as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/41f76e800a83fdbf548fb4a281d338f4.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{(p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A \\times B & = & A & \\cdot & B\\end{vmatrix} = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n$(A \\times B) \\times B$\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B$, as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/12cd1413549bdde65ed735cf3e17b99f.png)\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}(A \\times B) \\times B & = & A \\times B & \\cdot & B\\end{vmatrix} = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054349 1145 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{ p,q \\}$ with $\\{ 4,9 \\},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/69ff8391a11b7bfa221b6b303f31551288bd969bdeed778fb05d3b7094e027ae.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{ (p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A \\times B| = |A| \\cdot |B| = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A \\times B) \\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/5f4a74a98f9df15273d59131eadc1155ff2d1105e1027e7d10b7e2366102e046.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A \\times B) \\times B| = |A \\times B| \\cdot |B| = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054350 1100 "If $C = \\{ 3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D  =\\{ 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\},$ what is the cardinality of $C \\times D?$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054350 1100 "If $C = \\{3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D =\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\}$, what is the cardinality of $C \\times D$?" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054350 1145 "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D$, the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}C \\times D & = & C & \\cdot & D\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}C \\cdot D & &= & C & \\cdot & D\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054350 1145 "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D,$ the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n| C \\times D | = |C| \\cdot |D|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|C\\times D| & =|C|\\cdot |D| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054351 1100 "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}$." 13194139534746 false] [17592186054351 1100 "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}.$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054351 1145 "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA^2 = A \\times A\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/144ff19e358a073bcea04a9719793455.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/4f01eda6f1609be0d129be13ac2d565c.png)" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054351 1145 "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A^2 = A \\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/4646235c2009dc45d7d09a52162ec0bdf929d122f450791ae9917b3ad8ef5a43.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/8c9647361885913d2534fefb49a878c2e365cf5be36d1bb4c059d95344dd1a60.png)" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307257 13194139534746 true] [17592186054546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307264 13194139534746 true] [17592186054546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307278 13194139534746 true] [17592186054546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307285 13194139534746 true] [17592186054547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307250 13194139534746 true] [17592186054547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307306 13194139534746 true] [17592186054547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307313 13194139534746 true] [17592186054547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307320 13194139534746 true] [17592186054548 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307243 13194139534746 true] [17592186054548 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307327 13194139534746 true] [17592186054548 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307334 13194139534746 true] [17592186054548 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307341 13194139534746 true] [17592186054549 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307271 13194139534746 true] [17592186054549 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307292 13194139534746 true] [17592186054549 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307299 13194139534746 true] [17592186054906 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534746 true] [17592186054906 1100 "For the sets $A = {2, 4, 6}$, $B = {x, y}$, and $C = A \\cdot B$, determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n1. $(2, 4) ∈ C$\n2. $(2, x) ∈ C$\n3. $(3, y) ∈ C$" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054906 1100 "For the sets $A={2,4,6},$ $B={x,y},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(2,4)\\in C$\n- $(2,x)\\in C$\n- $(3,y)\\in C$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054906 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/df4277feac4c4e838538f941aa0a07d087beb9005d8f7a8347dadc1da7fe975e.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(2,x),(2,y),(4,x),(4,y),(6,x),(6,y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534746 true] [17592186054909 1100 "What is the cardinality of $Z \\cdot Y$ for $Z = {1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36}$ and $Y = {2, 3, 7, 9}$?" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054909 1100 "What is the cardinality of $Z\\times Y$ for $Z={1,4,9,16,25,36}$ and $Y={2,3,7,9}?$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054909 1145 "For any two finite sets $Z$ and $Y,$ the cardinality of $Z\\times Y$ is\n\n$$\n|Z\\times Y|=|Z|\\cdot |Y|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|Z\\times Y| & =|Z|\\cdot |Y| \\\\  & =6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054982 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534746 true] [17592186054982 1100 "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A \\cdot B$ for $A = {1, 10, 100}$ and $B = {a, b}$?" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054982 1100 "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A={1,10,100}$ and $B={a,b}?$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054982 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/aac2b461bef7d5e1a7ae6e4c7b8b50ebeff86f14b45fc3f929a56ebc870e63c3.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/dd425d5211861a8c1b7944bd787bba5272a4640a61eb39ff63a477741c16fc93.png)" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534746 true] [17592186054984 1100 "If $A = {0, 1, 2}$ and $B = {x, y}$, which of the following elements belong to $(A \\cdot B) \\cdot A$?\n\n1. $(1, y, 2)$\n2. $((1, y), 2)$\n3. $((y, 1), y)$" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054984 1100 "If $A={0,1,2}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\n- $(1,y,2)$\n- $((1,y),2)$\n- $((y,1),y)$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054984 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534746 true] [17592186054985 1100 "If $A = {-1, 1}$ and $B = {u, v}$, which of the following elements belong to $B \\cdot B \\cdot A$?\n\n1. $(v, v, 1)$\n2. $((v, v), 1)$\n3. $(1, v, u)$" 13194139534746 false] [17592186054985 1100 "If $A={-1,1}$ and $B={u,v},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,v,1)$\n- $((v,v),1)$\n- $(1,v,u)$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186054985 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534746 true] [17592186307243 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307244 13194139534746 true] [17592186307243 1089 "q-105865" 13194139534746 true] [17592186307243 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534746 true] [17592186307243 1100 "What is the cardinality of $K\\times K$ for $K={k,l,m,n,o,p,r,s}?$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186307243 1113 #uuid "c7816ba3-5d85-5ea3-9b2e-ea25479bc6fc" 13194139534746 true] [17592186307243 1145 "For any two finite sets $A$ and $B,$ the cardinality of $A\\times B$ is\n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|K\\times K| & =|K|\\cdot |K| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534746 true] [17592186307244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307245 13194139534746 true] [17592186307244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307246 13194139534746 true] [17592186307244 100